<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation of Erasing across 4 Categories.</span> We average the results of all target concepts in each category across three metrics and compare our framework, SuMa, with state-of-the-art methods. For ASRs, successful attacks (ASR <math id="S4.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math> 20%) are in <span id="S4.T2.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#AD0000;">red</span>, and defeated attacks (ASR <math id="S4.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 20%) in <span id="S4.T2.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">green</span>. The data in each cell show the result of erasing a concept from pretrained SD1.4 / <span id="S4.T2.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SD1.5</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span> We evaluate SuMa across four tasks: subclass erasure, celebrity erasure, artistic style erasure, and instance erasure. For <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">subclass erasure</span>, we focus on a subset of ImageNette <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> known for its distinctive features. Three subclasses are selected for the test, including <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">English Springer Dog</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Garbage Truck</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Golf Ball</span>. For <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">celebrity erasure</span>, we select 3 celebrities in the Celebrity-1000 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, including <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">David Beckham</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Elon Musk</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adam Lambert</span> . For <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">artistic style erasure</span>, following the approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, we focus on the style of <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Van Gogh</span>, which is both famous and straightforward to verify in outputs. For instance erasure, we conduct experiments on three copyrighted concepts: <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Grumpy Cat</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mickey Mouse</span>, and the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">R2D2 robot</span>. Note that our method is specifically designed to address the problem with narrow concepts, so it does not work as well for NSFW concepts. However, as mentioned earlier, our work is complementary to DUO, so it can be easily integrated with this method to completely erase all kinds of concepts, demonstrated in the Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E NSFW Concept Erasure ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline</span>. We compare SuMa with four baselines: two E-CEMs (CA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and MACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) and four C-CEMs (RACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, AdvUnlearn (AU) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, STEREO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, DUO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>). For CA, MACE, RACE, and AU, we use their public code and recommended settings to erase concepts. For STEREO and DUO, we re-implement their methods using paper recommendations and verify consistency with reported results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Circumventing Methods.</span> We evaluate the robustness of our method under CCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and UnlearnDiff (UD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. For CCE, we use a different set of images from the <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subspace Construction</span> stage to ensure the model didn’t overfit to this image set. For both attack methods, we use publicly available code to generate adversarial prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>
First, to measure <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">effectiveness</span>, for each erased model, we generate 5,000 images using prompts from the MS-COCO validation set. We then evaluate the model quality when generating such non-target prompts using <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP</span> scores (higher is better) and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FID</span> scores (lower is better). To measure <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">completeness</span>, in the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCE</span> attack, for each erased model, we execute the circumventing method to acquire the adversarial token <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then generate 200 images using a template prompt: <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“A photo of a </span>{<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>}.” For style erasure, we use the template <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“A painting in the style of </span>{<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>}<span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”</span>. In the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UD</span> attack, we execute its code on our erased model to get the adversarial prompt and pass the prompt to the erased model. We report the attack success rate (ASR), where a lower score indicates better performance, following the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. For subclass erasure tasks, we utilize <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ResNet-50</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, pretrained on <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ImageNet</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, to measure ASR. For celebrity detection, we use the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GIPHY Celebrity Detector (GCD)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> as the classifier. To evaluate artistic style, we follow the approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> by fine-tuning a <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ViT-based</span> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> on the <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WikiArt</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Finally, for instance detection, due to the lack of pretrained models or datasets, we use <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLAVA-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> with a template prompt: <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Does this image contain </span>{<span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">object</span>}<span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">? Answer Yes or No.”</span> for evaluation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05625v1/Ablation_Study_1.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/506;" width="548" height="506" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of Textual Inversion tokens at different numbers of TI training steps.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> We use SD1.4 and SD1.5 as the pretrained model. In all experiments, we fine-tune all cross-attention layers. In the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subspace Construction</span> stage, we use the same setting as CA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> for concept erasion. Training TI is set for 500 steps, with the early step to get token <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle v\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle v\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> set to 50, the initial token is <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">toy</span>, with Adam optimization with learning rate, is 2e-6. For the <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subspace Elimination</span> stage, we fine-tune most objects for 500 steps, except for subclass objects, which require 750 steps to ensure model safety. We use the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 2e-5. All experiments are done on 2 NVIDIA A100 GPUs.</p>
</div>
