<td id="S4.T4.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.30</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.26</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T4.3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">✓</span></th>
<td id="S4.T4.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ASR: 0.12</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ASR: 0.09</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ASR: 0.05</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">ASR: 0.17</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FID: 18.33</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FID: 17.94</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FID: 17.18</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FID: 22.34</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.3.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.3.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.31</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.31</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.33</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.3.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.3.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CLIP: 0.29</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:129%;">Ablation Study for the need of Subspace Regulization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative Results</span> are provided in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Reference Subspace Selection for Narrow Concepts ‣ 3 Method ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Fig. <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="Figure 4(a) ‣ Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Reference Subspace Selection for Narrow Concepts ‣ 3 Method ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> shows results of these models against the CCE attack, and we include qualitative results for UD in the Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Additional Qualitative Results ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. CA, MACE, RACE, and AdvUnlearn are highly vulnerable to this attack. STEREO completely removes these concepts, but at the cost of image quality (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>6</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> column in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="Figure 4(b) ‣ Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Reference Subspace Selection for Narrow Concepts ‣ 3 Method ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>). Our method successfully converts “Springer Dog” to “Dog,” “Elon Musk” to a general person, and “Grumpy Cat” to “Cat toy,” while maintaining image quality comparable to other E-CEM methods (last column in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="Figure 4(b) ‣ Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Reference Subspace Selection for Narrow Concepts ‣ 3 Method ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multiple Concepts Erasure.</span> We provide results for erasing multiple concepts in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We test the ASR with the <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCE</span> attack and evaluate two settings: Iterative Erase (IE) and Simultaneous Erase (SE). <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IE</span> means we fine-tune the weights after erasing one concept to erase others. <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SE</span> sums all <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> to erase all concepts in one fine-tuning, with 1000 steps fine-tuning for this experiment. In both settings, our method defends against <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCE</span> and maintains model utility. With <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SE</span>, we achieve better FID scores, as summing <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves a broader range of knowledge from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{reg}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
