</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="A4.T7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of ASRs when applying <math id="A4.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> only versus <math id="A4.T7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}+L_{CA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}+L_{CA}</annotation></semantics></math>. The experiments with Identity and Artistic Style Erasure, in which the ASRs when using <math id="A4.T7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> only are larger than 50%, are in <span id="A4.T7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide an ablation study by applying <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> alone and in combination with <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{CA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{CA}</annotation></semantics></math>, reporting the Attack Success Rate (ASR) in Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D Modify Cross-Attn Only ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. In general, compared to existing E-CEM methods, applying <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> alone achieves lower ASRs. In most concepts, the ASR is smaller than 50%, except for the <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity</span> and <span id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Artistic Style</span> categories, where it performs significantly worse than normal. Furthermore, as shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix D Modify Cross-Attn Only ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the TI token for the <span id="A4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity</span> category from model <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (fine-tuned using <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> alone) generates the target concept only when conditioned on <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and fails to generate the target concept when conditioned on <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (the original model). Interestingly, this phenomenon is observed exclusively in the <span id="A4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity</span> category and not in others. In our opinion, this happens because Textual Inversion (TI) acts like a process that collects all the residual knowledge of the model about concepts closely related to the target concept and reconstructs the target concept. For the <span id="A4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity</span> category, TI tokens are more sensitive and constrained on <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to this phenomenon. When combining <math id="A4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{CA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{CA}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math> acts merely as a tool to protect the fine-tuned model from further CCE attacks. As mentioned in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, applying the erasing method <math id="A4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> iteratively can completely erase the target concept, though this results in a significant decrease in image quality. We hypothesize that, intuitively, applying method <math id="A4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> iteratively will help us identify all concepts that can be used to reconstruct the target concept during the process of finding TI tokens. When combined with <math id="A4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{sub}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{sub}</annotation></semantics></math>, this method will guide the model to converge to a point where it can completely erase the target concept, with minimal weight modification. Figure <a href="#A8.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix H More Visualizations of Textual Inversion ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> illustrates the output of TI at different levels of applying method <math id="A4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to erase the TI of the target concept.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>NSFW Concept Erasure</h2>

<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05625v1/TI_Multi_Step.png" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/253;" width="685" height="253" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Output of the TI token at different TI training steps.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A5.T8.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:233.5pt;height:64pt;vertical-align:-30.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-16.7pt,4.6pt) scale(0.874790157528541,0.874790157528541) ;">
<table id="A5.T8.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T8.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T8.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T8.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Mehod</span></th>
