<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span><span id="A5.T8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result of erasing “Nudity” concept from SD1.4.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Experimental Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, SuMa doesn’t work very well for NSFW concepts, such as the ”Nudity” concept. The reason is that during Textual Inversion fine-tuning, the early steps for the “Nudity” concept directly converge to this concept, as shown in Figure <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix E NSFW Concept Erasure ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, making it impossible to construct a reference subspace for this concept using our proposed approach. Instead, we propose two alternative solutions. First, we could employ the Pushing Approach, as described in Section <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Subspace Pushing ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, because this approach does not require a reference subspace. However, in our experiments, we found that while this approach is able to keep the model safe under CCE and UD attacks (Following previous work, we use the Nudenet Detector to compute ASR
), its FID and CLIP scores significantly decrease, as shown in Table <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix E NSFW Concept Erasure ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Second, we found that after erasing one narrow concept based on our method, we can still apply DUO afterward, with results very close to applying DUO directly, as shown in Table <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix E NSFW Concept Erasure ‣ SuMa: A Subspace Mapping Approach for Robust and Effective Concept Erasure in Text-to-Image Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. So, in conclusion, our work could be combined with DUO to eliminate all kinds of concepts and advance the development of a safe text-to-image model.</p>
</div>
</section>
